Q1 :
ANS 1:Frontend development is the part of web development that focuses on the user interface (UI) of a website or web application. It is everything that a user can see and interact with on the screen. Frontend developers mainly use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to design attractive and responsive web pages.

Example

When you open Amazon, the search bar, product images, navigation menu, and "Add to Cart" button are all part of the frontend because users can see and use them.

Backend development is the server-side part of a website that works behind the scenes. It is responsible for processing user requests, handling business logic, connecting to the database, and sending the required data to the frontend. Common backend technologies include Java, Python, PHP, Node.js, and databases like MySQL.

Example

When a customer logs into Amazon or places an order, the backend checks the username and password, saves the order details in the database, and processes the payment.

Full-stack development is a combination of frontend and backend development. A full-stack developer can design the user interface as well as develop the server-side logic and manage the database.

Example

If a developer builds an online shopping website from start to finish, including the product pages, login system, shopping cart, payment processing, and database, then that developer is working as a full-stack developer.


Q2 :
ANS 2:The Client-Server Model is a communication model used in web architecture where the client sends a request to the server, and the server processes the request and sends the required response back to the client. The client is usually a web browser, while the server stores the website files and data.

This model allows users to access websites and web applications through the internet efficiently.

Working of the Client-Server Model
The client-server model works in the following steps:
1:The user opens a web browser and enters the website URL.
2:The browser (client) sends an HTTP request to the web server.
3:The web server receives the request and processes it.
4:If required, the server retrieves data from the database.
5:The server sends the requested web page (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, etc.) back to the client.
6:Finally, the browser displays the web page to the user.
